1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,272
What if your heart suddenly beat

2
00:00:02,272 --> 00:00:03,289
five times faster?

3
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,114
At rest, it beats about seventy

4
00:00:06,114 --> 00:00:08,100
times a minute. Five times that

5
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,494
is three hundred fifty.

6
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:12,079
Speeding up is normal. Hard exercise

7
00:00:12,079 --> 00:00:13,766
can push a healthy young adult's

8
00:00:13,766 --> 00:00:15,532
heart to around one hundred ninety

9
00:00:15,532 --> 00:00:16,403
beats per minute.

10
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:19,168
But at three hundred fifty, each

11
00:00:19,168 --> 00:00:21,055
beat lasts under two-tenths of a

12
00:00:21,055 --> 00:00:23,159
second. The chambers barely have time

13
00:00:23,159 --> 00:00:24,521
to refill between beats.

14
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:27,470
Faster doesn't mean more blood. With

15
00:00:27,470 --> 00:00:29,579
less filling, each beat pushes out

16
00:00:29,579 --> 00:00:31,412
less, and blood pressure drops.

17
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,242
Your brain notices first. Dizziness, tunnel

18
00:00:35,242 --> 00:00:37,978
vision, and fainting could follow within

19
00:00:37,978 --> 00:00:38,506
seconds.

20
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:41,740
Worse: the heart feeds itself while

21
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:43,967
relaxing. Now it barely does, and

22
00:00:43,967 --> 00:00:46,382
its rhythm can collapse into chaos.

23
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:49,710
A hummingbird's heart can top one

24
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:52,019
thousand beats a minute, but it's

25
00:00:52,019 --> 00:00:53,764
built for it. Yours isn't.

26
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,324
Your heart doesn't just beat. It

27
00:00:57,324 --> 00:00:59,668
keeps time. Follow for more what-ifs.

